# FacetOS Domain Startup, Terminal, Identity, and Authentication Specification

## 1. Scope

This document defines the initial FacetOS model for:

* seats and terminals;
* domain terminal assignment;
* native and POSIX domain personalities;
* native and POSIX terminal views;
* initial process startup;
* pure POSIX PID 1 startup;
* authentication sources;
* principals and human users;
* authentication results;
* domain-local session creation;
* initial login flow.

The design intentionally separates:

```text
physical/system resources
        ↓
FacetOS resource identity
        ↓
domain assignment
        ↓
personality-specific presentation
        ↓
process/session
```

A domain-local representation of a resource must not be assumed to reveal the underlying implementation of that resource.

---

# 2. Example `facet.toml`

```toml id="wt8hn7"
[facet]
version = 1


# ===========================================================================
# Logging sinks
# ===========================================================================

[[logging_sinks]]
name = "debug"
type = "platform.sel4.debug"
required = true


# ===========================================================================
# Authentication sources
#
# Authentication sources provide IAuthService.
#
# Domains consume authentication sources by name and do not need to know
# how the authentication provider is implemented.
# ===========================================================================

[[authentication_sources]]
name = "system"
provider_domain = 0
type = "local"
required = true


# ===========================================================================
# Seats
#
# Seats describe system-side terminal inventory.
#
# Terminal names in this namespace may carry system-side meaning.
# In particular, ttyS# denotes a serial console.
# ===========================================================================

[[seats]]
name = "seat0"
type = "serial"
terminals = [
    "ttyS0"
]

[[seats]]
name = "seat1"
type = "local"
terminals = [
    "tty1",
    "tty2",
    "tty3",
    "tty4",
    "tty5"
]


# ===========================================================================
# Domain 0: system
#
# Native domain.
#
# Its assigned serial console receives a native FacetOS view and starts
# FacetLogin directly.
# ===========================================================================

[[domains]]
id = 0
name = "system"
personality = "native"
domain_manager = "local"
authentication_source = "system"

logging_sinks = [
    { name = "debug", level = "debug" }
]

terminals = [
    {
        terminal = "seat0.ttyS0",
        view = "native",
        initial_process = "/FacetOS/FacetLogin"
    }
]


# ===========================================================================
# Domain 1: mixed native domain
#
# The domain itself uses the native personality.
#
# tty1 receives a native FacetOS login environment.
#
# tty2 receives a POSIX compatibility view and starts /bin/login.
#
# Both remain terminal/process environments inside the same native domain.
# ===========================================================================

[[domains]]
id = 1
name = "example-child"
personality = "native"
domain_manager = "none"
authentication_source = "system"

logging_sinks = [
    { name = "debug", level = "info" }
]

terminals = [
    {
        terminal = "seat1.tty1",
        view = "native",
        initial_process = "/FacetOS/FacetLogin"
    },
    {
        terminal = "seat1.tty2",
        view = "posix",
        initial_process = "/bin/login"
    }
]


# ===========================================================================
# Domain 2: pure POSIX domain
#
# FacetOS constructs a POSIX environment for the entire domain.
#
# There is no FacetOS-managed per-terminal initial process.
#
# Each assigned FacetOS terminal is explicitly mapped to a domain-local
# POSIX device name.
#
# Domain-local device names DO NOT describe the underlying hardware.
#
# For example, /dev/ttyS0 below is backed by seat1.tty5. The name ttyS0 has
# serial semantics inside this POSIX environment only because the domain
# chooses to present it under that name. seat1.tty5 itself is not serial.
#
# FacetOS starts exactly one initial process: PID 1.
# Everything after that is POSIX userspace policy.
# ===========================================================================

[[domains]]
id = 2
name = "posix-example"
personality = "posix"
domain_manager = "none"
authentication_source = "system"

pid1 = "/sbin/init"

logging_sinks = [
    { name = "debug", level = "info" }
]

terminals = [
    { device = "tty1",  terminal = "seat1.tty3" },
    { device = "tty2",  terminal = "seat1.tty4" },
    { device = "ttyS0", terminal = "seat1.tty5" }
]
```

---

# 3. Seats and System-Side Terminals

A seat represents a collection of related system-side interactive resources.

For v1, the configuration explicitly describes its terminals:

```toml id="sawmdg"
[[seats]]
name = "seat1"
type = "local"
terminals = [
    "tty1",
    "tty2",
    "tty3",
    "tty4",
    "tty5"
]
```

A system-side terminal is identified using:

```text id="a2lv9h"
<seat>.<terminal>
```

For example:

```text id="7usjkn"
seat0.ttyS0
seat1.tty1
seat1.tty5
```

These names belong to the FacetOS system namespace.

They are not domain-local device names.

## 3.1 System-side naming semantics

Outside domains, conventional FacetOS terminal names may describe the underlying terminal class.

In particular:

```text id="i1esvk"
ttyS#
```

means a serial console.

Therefore:

```text id="y9ctao"
seat0.ttyS0
```

is a system-side serial console.

This rule applies to the system-side seat/terminal inventory.

It does not apply to names chosen by a domain personality.

---

# 4. Domain Personalities

FacetOS initially defines two domain personalities:

```text id="sm6xkt"
native
posix
```

A personality defines the fundamental execution environment presented to processes within that domain.

Personality is distinct from the view attached to an individual terminal.

---

# 5. Native Domains

A native domain is declared using:

```toml id="ibzvml"
personality = "native"
```

FacetOS directly manages the initial process associated with each assigned terminal.

Each terminal assignment therefore specifies:

```text id="qx5d5i"
terminal
view
initial_process
```

For example:

```toml id="at0qt9"
{
    terminal = "seat1.tty1",
    view = "native",
    initial_process = "/FacetOS/FacetLogin"
}
```

or:

```toml id="9m4uob"
{
    terminal = "seat1.tty2",
    view = "posix",
    initial_process = "/bin/login"
}
```

---

# 6. Terminal Views

The terminal `view` controls the environment presented to the process initially attached to that terminal.

Initially:

```text id="9q2p2j"
native
posix
```

This does not change the personality of the containing domain.

Thus Domain 1 is:

```text id="vzcw1e"
Domain 1
personality = native

    seat1.tty1
        |
        +-- native view
        |
        `-- /FacetOS/FacetLogin

    seat1.tty2
        |
        +-- POSIX view
        |
        `-- /bin/login
```

Both terminal processes remain part of Domain 1.

A POSIX terminal view is a compatibility environment within an otherwise native domain.

---

# 7. Pure POSIX Domains

A pure POSIX domain is declared using:

```toml id="5v0cmt"
personality = "posix"
```

Its startup model differs fundamentally from a native domain.

FacetOS does not launch one initial process for every terminal.

Instead:

1. FacetOS creates the domain.
2. The POSIX personality receives the domain's assigned resources.
3. Assigned Facet terminals are mapped into the domain-local POSIX namespace.
4. FacetOS starts one configured executable as PID 1.
5. PID 1 becomes responsible for POSIX userspace startup.

The configuration therefore contains:

```toml id="pcwyeb"
pid1 = "/sbin/init"
```

rather than per-terminal `initial_process` fields.

Conceptually:

```text id="oq19mf"
dominit
   |
   +-- create Domain 2
   |
   +-- create POSIX environment
   |
   +-- map terminals
   |
   +-- supply other assigned resources
   |
   `-- start /sbin/init
             PID 1
               |
               v
         POSIX userspace
```

FacetOS does not require PID 1 to behave like any particular Unix init system.

---

# 8. Pure POSIX Terminal Mapping

Pure POSIX terminal assignments contain two independent names:

```text id="i7df8u"
device
terminal
```

For example:

```toml id="ekbl8q"
{ device = "ttyS0", terminal = "seat1.tty5" }
```

means:

```text id="o74mb4"
Domain-local POSIX device: /dev/ttyS0

Underlying FacetOS terminal: seat1.tty5
```

For the example Domain 2:

```text id="otv22w"
FacetOS terminal       Domain-local POSIX device

seat1.tty3       ────► /dev/tty1
seat1.tty4       ────► /dev/tty2
seat1.tty5       ────► /dev/ttyS0
```

The two namespaces are independent.

The mapping must always be explicit.

---

# 9. Hardware Meaning Stops at the Domain Boundary

This distinction is fundamental.

## Outside domains

System-side terminal names may carry hardware meaning.

Specifically:

```text id="rjgf8v"
ttyS# = serial console
```

Thus:

```text id="dyp8pq"
seat0.ttyS0
```

really is a serial console.

## Inside domains

Domain-local names are logical names belonging to that domain's personality.

They do not imply anything about physical hardware.

Therefore:

```text id="fvd9re"
/dev/ttyS0 -> seat1.tty5
```

is completely valid.

`seat1.tty5` is not a serial console merely because Domain 2 calls it `ttyS0`.

Likewise this could be valid:

```text id="i5l7bl"
/dev/tty7 -> seat0.ttyS0
```

The underlying FacetOS terminal remains serial, while the POSIX domain chooses to call it `tty7`.

Therefore:

> System-side names describe FacetOS resources.
>
> Domain-local names describe the personality's presentation of those resources.
>
> Hardware properties must never be inferred backwards from a domain-local name.

---

# 10. POSIX PID 1 Responsibilities

FacetOS starts only the configured PID 1 in a pure POSIX domain.

For Domain 2:

```text id="wuhy69"
/sbin/init
```

is started as PID 1.

PID 1 may subsequently choose to launch:

```text id="j6fb4u"
getty
/bin/login
shells
daemons
service managers
network services
```

For example:

```text id="8j0fb3"
PID 1
  |
  +-- getty /dev/tty1
  |      |
  |      `-- /bin/login
  |
  +-- getty /dev/tty2
  |      |
  |      `-- /bin/login
  |
  `-- getty /dev/ttyS0
         |
         `-- /bin/login
```

This behaviour is not part of the FacetOS domain-startup contract.

A POSIX domain may choose not to run any interactive login service at all.

---

# 11. Authentication Sources

Authentication is represented as a service.

It is not intrinsically owned by FacetLogin or by the POSIX personality.

A configured authentication source provides:

```text id="e9f7aj"
IAuthService
```

Example:

```toml id="7b84hq"
[[authentication_sources]]
name = "system"
provider_domain = 0
type = "local"
required = true
```

Domains request authentication sources by name:

```toml id="r1z76k"
authentication_source = "system"
```

Dominit resolves that relationship and supplies the appropriate authentication capability.

Consumers use `IAuthService`.

They do not need to know where or how it is implemented.

---

# 12. Authentication Delegation

A domain may manage its own authentication or consume authentication supplied by another domain.

Conceptually:

```text id="6c6y84"
Domain 0
   |
   +-- authentication implementation
   |
   `-- IAuthService
          |
          +----------► Domain 0
          |
          +----------► Domain 1
          |
          `----------► Domain 2
```

In the example, Domain 0 provides the `system` authentication source and all three domains consume it.

This means they share an authentication authority.

It does not mean they share authorization policy.

---

# 13. Authentication vs Authorization

Authentication answers:

> Who is this?

Authorization/session construction answers:

> What authority does this authenticated identity receive in this domain?

These must remain separate.

Thus:

```text id="63dkx9"
              IAuthService
                   |
                   | authenticate
                   v
       IAuthenticatedPrincipal
                   |
          +--------+--------+
          |                 |
          v                 v
 Domain 0 security    Domain 1 security
      policy               policy
          |                 |
          v                 v
    ISession A         ISession B
```

The same authenticated principal may receive different capabilities in different domains.

Trusting an authentication provider must not implicitly delegate the receiving domain's authorization policy.

---

# 14. IPrincipal

`IPrincipal` represents an identity recognised by the FacetOS security model.

A principal need not represent a human.

Potential examples include:

```text id="rb40kk"
human
service
device
agent
remote identity
```

The initial interface should remain small:

```text id="qug4qe"
IPrincipal

    GetID()
    GetName()
```

The ID is the stable principal identity.

A UUID is appropriate for this purpose.

## Security rule

Possession of an `IPrincipal` is not authority to act as that principal.

It must therefore be safe for ordinary code to hold references to principals for purposes such as:

```text id="2ml9rp"
ownership
metadata
display
auditing
ACL entries
```

without thereby acquiring the principal's filesystem/session authority.

---

# 15. IHumanUser

A principal representing a human may additionally expose:

```text id="bttkjv"
IHumanUser
```

This follows the FacetOS object/interface model:

```text id="o1a3br"
Human principal object
    |
    +-- IPrincipal
    +-- IHumanUser
    `-- IPronouns
```

`IHumanUser` initially contains:

```text id="jj5rg6"
FullName
Pronouns
HomePath
DefaultShell
```

`HomePath` and `DefaultShell` remain strings for v1.

Do not prematurely replace them with PolyFS object IDs, capabilities, executable objects, or other speculative abstractions.

---

# 16. IPronouns

`IPronouns` stores the forms needed for basic English-language pronoun handling.

It contains:

```text id="o4wqzp"
Subject
Object
PossessiveDeterminer
PossessivePronoun
Reflexive
ShortForm
```

The grammatical forms take an agreement parameter.

Define:

```c id="4ppx79"
enum IPronouns_GrammaticalNumber {
    PRONOUN_AGREEMENT_SINGULAR = 1,
    PRONOUN_AGREEMENT_PLURAL   = 2,
};
```

Grammatical agreement does not necessarily describe the number of people represented.

Singular `they`, for example, may use plural grammatical agreement.

Conceptually:

```text id="cy22bk"
GetSubject(agreement)
SetSubject(agreement, value)

GetObject(agreement)
SetObject(agreement, value)

GetPossessiveDeterminer(agreement)
SetPossessiveDeterminer(agreement, value)

GetPossessivePronoun(agreement)
SetPossessivePronoun(agreement, value)

GetReflexive(agreement)
SetReflexive(agreement, value)

GetShortForm()
SetShortForm(value)
```

Example:

```text id="evp8wz"
subject                = she
object                 = her
possessive determiner  = her
possessive pronoun     = hers
reflexive              = herself
short form             = she/her/hers
```

---

# 17. IAuthService

`IAuthService` performs authentication.

Conceptually:

```text id="a34ih7"
Authenticate(
    username,
    credential_type,
    credential_subtype,
    credential_blob
)
    -> IAuthenticatedPrincipal
```

The precise IDL form must follow existing FacetOS conventions.

Credentials consist of:

```text id="8m0hax"
credential type
credential subtype
opaque credential blob
```

Initially, the only required broad credential type is:

```text id="4ydh5i"
Password
```

Additional credential types must not be invented until needed.

Password subtypes similarly belong to their own subtype namespace.

The credential blob is interpreted according to its type and subtype.

---

# 18. IAuthenticatedPrincipal

Successful authentication returns an:

```text id="fzswfr"
IAuthenticatedPrincipal
```

This is distinct from `IPrincipal`.

Its minimum operation is:

```text id="9ir74b"
GetPrincipal() -> IPrincipal
```

Semantically:

```text id="7h22nu"
IPrincipal
    "this identity exists"

IAuthenticatedPrincipal
    "a trusted authentication authority has
     successfully authenticated this identity"
```

`IAuthenticatedPrincipal` is therefore security-sensitive.

It must not be forgeable merely by obtaining an `IPrincipal`.

Authentication provenance must remain sufficient for the receiving security authority to determine whether the result came from an authentication source it trusts.

The exact low-level provenance mechanism is not defined here.

Do not invent signed bearer tokens or another cryptographic protocol unless they become necessary.

---

# 19. ISecurityManager

Each domain capable of creating authenticated sessions has an:

```text id="x4p20g"
ISecurityManager
```

This represents domain-local authorization/session policy.

Its core operation is:

```text id="v91p9r"
CreateSession(
    IAuthenticatedPrincipal authentication
)
    -> ISession
```

The security manager must accept authentication results only from authentication sources trusted/configured for that domain.

The security manager decides what capabilities the resulting session receives.

Thus the authentication source establishes identity while the security manager establishes authority.

---

# 20. ISession

`ISession` represents one particular authenticated security context.

A principal may have multiple sessions.

For example:

```text id="bqet2b"
               Gwen principal
                    |
            +-------+-------+
            |               |
            v               v
        Session A       Session B
            |               |
        local tty         remote
            |               |
       broad access     restricted
```

Both sessions represent the same principal but may possess different capabilities.

Initially, `ISession` should expose:

```text id="abukrs"
GetPrincipal()
```

and eventually the process-spawning operation required to start programs inside that session.

The exact process-spawning API should follow the FacetOS process model rather than being invented solely for `ISession`.

---

# 21. Native FacetLogin

FacetLogin is the native interactive login program:

```text id="f9sf8j"
/FacetOS/FacetLogin
```

It receives the capabilities required for login, conceptually:

```text id="20aq3a"
IVirtualTerminal
IAuthService
ISecurityManager
```

The flow is:

```text id="74o5xg"
FacetLogin
    |
    | collect username/credentials
    v
IAuthService::Authenticate(...)
    |
    v
IAuthenticatedPrincipal
    |
    +-- GetPrincipal()
    |       |
    |       v
    |   IPrincipal
    |       |
    |       +-- query IHumanUser
    |
    v
ISecurityManager::CreateSession(...)
    |
    v
ISession
    |
    v
user's DefaultShell
```

FacetLogin does not itself determine filesystem authority or other domain policy.

That belongs to `ISecurityManager`.

---

# 22. Native Domain with POSIX Login View

Domain 1 demonstrates a different case:

```toml id="r1esq8"
{
    terminal = "seat1.tty2",
    view = "posix",
    initial_process = "/bin/login"
}
```

The domain itself remains native.

The POSIX view provides the compatibility environment required by `/bin/login`.

Conceptually:

```text id="9i5du6"
/bin/login
     |
     v
POSIX authentication compatibility
     |
     v
IAuthService
     |
     v
IAuthenticatedPrincipal
     |
     v
ISecurityManager
     |
     v
ISession
     |
     v
POSIX session representation
```

POSIX concepts such as:

```text id="o9p2km"
uid
gid
home
shell
process credentials
```

belong to the POSIX compatibility layer.

They must not be added to `IPrincipal` merely because POSIX requires them.

---

# 23. Authentication in a Pure POSIX Domain

A pure POSIX domain receives access to its configured authentication source, but FacetOS itself starts only PID 1.

Thus Domain 2 begins approximately as:

```text id="6ffu38"
Domain 2

/dev/tty1
/dev/tty2
/dev/ttyS0
authentication integration
other assigned resources

        |
        v

/sbin/init
PID 1
```

What happens next is controlled by POSIX userspace.

For example:

```text id="r7x8sw"
/sbin/init
    |
    +-- getty
    |      |
    |      `-- /bin/login
    |
    +-- getty
    |      |
    |      `-- /bin/login
    |
    `-- services
```

When POSIX userspace requests authentication, the POSIX personality may bridge the expected POSIX authentication API onto the configured `IAuthService`.

FacetOS does not require `/bin/login` to exist.

FacetOS does not automatically create gettys.

FacetOS does not dictate how PID 1 manages interactive users.

---

# 24. Initial Bootstrap Authentication

The initial authentication implementation may be deliberately minimal for development.

For bootstrap/testing:

* arbitrary non-`root` usernames may use placeholder behaviour;
* a `root` principal exists;
* the provisional root password may be `facet`.

This is development scaffolding, not the permanent security design.

Do not build a large account-management or cryptographic password framework merely to support this bootstrap state.

---

# 25. Configuration Validation

## 25.1 Global requirements

Configuration must contain:

* domain ID 0;
* at least one seat;
* at least one logging sink.

IDs and names required to be unique must be validated before materialisation.

References must be resolved before domain construction.

---

## 25.2 Seat validation

Seat names must be unique.

Terminal names must be unique within a seat.

A terminal reference:

```text id="sjixrb"
seat1.tty3
```

must resolve to both:

```text id="4k86fi"
seat1
tty3
```

in the configured inventory.

System-side `ttyS#` semantics remain meaningful.

---

## 25.3 Domain validation

Domain IDs must be unique.

Domain 0 must exist.

Every domain must have:

* at least one logging sink;
* at least one assigned terminal;
* valid logging-sink references;
* valid terminal references;
* a valid authentication-source reference where authentication is configured.

Terminal assignment must obey system exclusivity rules.

---

## 25.4 Native-domain validation

For:

```toml id="0sr6na"
personality = "native"
```

every terminal assignment must contain:

```text id="9j2a4c"
terminal
view
initial_process
```

`view` must identify a supported terminal view.

Initially:

```text id="nl5p8l"
native
posix
```

A native domain must not specify domain-level `pid1`.

---

## 25.5 POSIX-domain validation

For:

```toml id="u95smm"
personality = "posix"
```

`pid1` is mandatory.

Every terminal mapping must contain:

```text id="m5j6ya"
device
terminal
```

The system-side terminal reference must resolve.

Domain-local `device` names must be unique within the domain.

The validator must not infer hardware type from `device`.

The validator must not infer `device` from the system-side terminal name.

Therefore this is valid:

```toml id="v84hjo"
{ device = "ttyS0", terminal = "seat1.tty5" }
```

and must not be rejected because `seat1.tty5` is not serial.

---

# 26. Configuration Resolution Boundary

The existing configuration implementation boundary must be preserved:

```text id="fkp87f"
facet.toml bytes
      |
      v
portable parser
      |
      v
resolved typed configuration
      |
      v
dominit0-only materializer
      |
      v
generated interface structs
      |
      v
runtime/domain construction
```

The seL4-specific adapter supplies only the optional configuration byte span.

The portable configuration layer performs parsing, validation and reference resolution.

It must not construct seL4-specific runtime objects.

The dominit0-only materializer converts already-resolved typed data into the generated structures required by dominit0.

---

# 27. Resolved Terminal Representation

The portable typed configuration must preserve system-side and domain-local identity separately.

For a native terminal assignment, resolved information includes conceptually:

```text id="l5wzct"
resolved seat
resolved terminal
view
initial process
```

For a POSIX-domain terminal mapping:

```text id="tyy6yk"
domain-local device name
resolved seat
resolved system terminal
```

For example:

```toml id="x5pcn9"
{ device = "ttyS0", terminal = "seat1.tty5" }
```

must remain conceptually:

```text id="2bymml"
device_name = "ttyS0"

system_terminal:
    seat = seat1
    terminal = tty5
```

These must never be collapsed into a single inferred name.

